schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Hypercert (ERC-1155 impact-claim token)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A digital token, built on the ERC-1155 multi-token standard on Ethereum,
    that captures who did what work, where, and during what timeframe, sold
    prospectively (before work, to raise capital) or retrospectively (after
    impact, to claim funding for completed work), and that can be split or
    merged so funders can support exactly the slice of impact they care
    about.
  source_traditions:
    - Hypercerts Foundation protocol design
    - ERC-1155 multi-token standard (Ethereum)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    The core mechanism described in impact-certificates-hypercerts.md for
    making retroactive-funding claims verifiable, permanent, and transferable.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - retroactive-public-goods-funding
    - quadratic-funding
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      A hypercert is a claim of work done, not a certified/audited proof —
      the article names "Evaluators" as the separate role that assesses
      impact claims and builds reputation for doing so, distinct from the
      creator and funder roles.
  tensions:
    - >-
      Split/merge flexibility for funders vs. the risk of double-counting or
      overlapping claims across split certificates if not tracked carefully.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    The article states hypercerts are "Built on the AT Protocol," which
    appears to be a factual error (AT Protocol is the Bluesky/social-web
    protocol; hypercerts are ERC-1155 tokens on Ethereum per the same
    article's "How It Works" section) — flagged separately as a signal
    rather than repeated here as fact.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/impact-certificates-hypercerts.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
